N,G|               The screen remains dark for a moment.
O|
M|               In the darkness WE HEAR the film's THEME MUSIC - a gentle 
M|               whirring purring noise. Nothing to be alarmed about. It 
M|               might be a small contented motor.
O|
T|               FADE IN:
O|
S|               EXT. A DESERTED STREET - NIGHT
O|
N,G|               LONG SHOT of the solitary figure of a WOMAN standing 
N|               professionally alone at the end of the street.
O|
N|               It is a bright, still night. We can HEAR the Woman whistling 
N|               'Stardust' merrily to herself.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA TRACKS around her. A Man's footsteps are overlaid.
O|
N|               We HEAR the Man start to whistle 'Stardust' under his breath - 
N|               haltingly at first, then in time with the Woman.
O|
N|               As we approach, she glances at us over her shoulder - then 
N|               turns round for a better look.
O|
N|               Her whistling stops - so, at the same moment, does the 
N|               man's.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT of DORA - a plump, attractive brunette - still 
N|               young enough to need two glances at the customers.
O|
N|               She smiles at us - and is pleased with the reception. She 
N|               hesitates for a long moment, weighing us up carefully... 
N|               and then - half defiantly, half expecting to be laughed 
N|               at.
O|
C|                                     DORA
U|                         It'll be two quid...
O|
N|               Evidently we have two quid.  
O|
N|               She beams with relief - throws her fur over her shoulders, 
N|               jerks her head towards the right - and sets off.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA TRACKS after her.  Overlaid is the sound of the 
N|               man's footsteps.
O|
N|               Dora resumes her whistling. So, under his breath, does the 
N|               man who is following her.
O|
S|               EXT. A DESERTED STREET - NIGHT
O|
N|               A wider street than the last - but just as empty.  
O|
N|               Dora sways her way towards a small house. CAMERA FOLLOWS 
N|               at a respectful distance.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA PANS from Dora's hips to an overflowing dustbin.  
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT of a man's hand throwing something into the 
N|               dustbin. It is an empty packet marked Kodak Film.  CAMERA 
N|               PANS to Dora's house. It stands next to a chemist shop.
O|
N|               Dora climbs the few steps which lead to her front door - 
N|               glances round at us encouragingly - then unlocks the door.
O|
S|               INT. HALLWAY OF DORA'S HOUSE - NIGHT
O|
N|               She switches on a light - and hurries up a flight of stairs. 
O|
N,G|               CAMERA TRACKS after her.
O|
N|               She changes her tune to 'Goodnight Sweetheart'... and so - 
N|               under his breath - does the Man who is following her.  
O|
N|               A woman with hair like a two-toned car comes down the 
N|               stairs, winks at Dora - looks at us for a moment with great 
N,G|               curiosity... winks... then passes out of camera.
O|
N,G|               Dora reaches the landing - we are close behind her.
O|
S|               INT. LANDING OF DORA'S HOUSE - NIGHT
O|
N|               Dora unlocks the door of her room - and goes inside.
O|
S|               INT. DORA'S ROOM - NIGHT
O|
N|               She switches on the light, throws her fur onto a chair, 
N|               lights the gas fire  then turns round.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT of Dora. She holds out her hand-smiling.
O|
N|               And suddenly. There is a gentle whirring purring sound.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA HOLDS ON Dora - she is staring at something with 
N|               great curiosity.
O|
N|               It turns quickly to bewilderment - and the bewilderment to 
N,G|               fear. She steps back from CAMERA - but CAMERA won't have 
N,G|               it.
O|
N|               Dora is now staring at something in horror - she opens her 
N|               mouth to scream - a shadow falls across her face.
O|
N|               The sound purrs on.
O|
T|                                                              FADE OUT: 
O|
T|               FADE IN:
O|
S,G|               BLACK AND WHITE FILM SEQUENCE
O|
N|               The solitary figure of Dora standing professionally alone 
N,G|               at the end of a street. WE SEE her turn towards CAMERA - 
N|               and smile at us.
O|
M,G|               We are watching her on a 16mm screen - projected in BLACK 
M,G|               AND WHITE.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA PULLS BACK to show the surround in natural colour.
O|
S|               INT. MARK'S ROOM - NIGHT
O|
N,G|               A darkened room in natural COLOUR. The movie screen images 
N,G|               are in BLACK AND WHITE.  
O|
N,G|               We can SEE the back of a Man's head as he bends intently 
N|               over a projector. He is watching Dora on the screen. He is 
N|               breathing quickly.
O|
N,G|               We see Dora's hips waggling their way home.
O|
N,G|               The Man raises his head, so that we cannot see the screen.
O|
N,G|               When he lowers it again, we see the Woman with the two-
N,G|               toned hair winking at Dora... then we see Dora throwing 
N,G|               her fur onto a chair - and turning towards us.
O|
N,G|               WE SEE her staring at something in bewilderment... then 
N,G|               backing away from CAMERA in fear.
O|
N|               WE HEAR the Man breathing as if at the end of a very long 
N|               race.
O|
N|               As Dora opens her mouth to scream, and a shadow falls across 
N,M|               her face - the title:
O|
M|                                      PEEPING TOM
O|
N|               Blots out what is happening to her.
O|
N,M,G|               OTHER CREDITS FOLLOW... behind them we can see Dora's hands 
N|               pushing something away.
O|
N,M|               Before the DIRECTOR'S CREDIT:
O|
T|                                                                 CUT TO:
O|
S|               EXT. A PARK - NIGHT
O|
N|               It is very dark.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT of a stack of deck chairs.
O|
N|               Two forms behind it - a MAN'S and a WOMAN'S.
O|
N|               They are intertwined and motionless... suddenly a brilliant 
N|               shaft of light is trained onto them.
O|
N|               Overlaid is a gentle, whirring sound.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA PANS quickly to a nearby tree.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT of the lens of a cine-camera - the motor 
N|               purring... a blinding spotlight.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA PANS to the couple - the whirring of the camera is 
N|               overlaid.
O|
N|               The Man leaps up - shielding his eyes against the light.
O|
N|               He advances towards the tree.
O|
C|                                     MAN
U|                         Hey, you peeping...
O|
N|               The spotlight goes out. There is the sound of footsteps 
N|               running away  and the night is at peace again.
O|
N,G,M|               And now WE SEE who DIRECTED the picture.
O|
