S|               INT. THE INNER ROOM - NIGHT
O|
N|               We can HEAR - faintly  the dance music from downstairs.  
N|               There is a click - and the walls are suddenly bathed in 
N|               diffused light, throwing the room into delicate shadow. 
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT of Helen looking round in amazement.
O|
N|               As certain events of possible interest are to take place 
N|               in this room, here, in detail, is what amazes Helen.
O|
N|               The room is the product of three rooms which have been 
N|               knocked into one. It is very large and extremely well 
N|               constructed (as we are soon to learn, it was originally a 
N|               laboratory). One half of the room is used for processing, 
N|               and the other half for filming - and for trade shows. The 
N|               two halves are lined by long shelves upon which are perched 
N|               all shapes and sizes of cameras, their spectacles glinting 
N|               in the light.
O|
N|               In the processing half two benches (a 'dry' bench and a 
N|               'wet' bench) face each other against opposite walls. There 
N|               are three sinks above the 'wet' bench and an outburst of 
N|               equipment above the 'dry'.
O|
N|               This part of the room is lit by two dark-room lamps frowning 
N|               in the ceiling above the benches.
O|
N|               The other part of the room has a window at the far end of 
N|               it. Heavy drapes are putted across it.
O|
N|               Mark's projector rests on a small table in front of the 
N|               16mm screen.  Two banks of floods and a variety of spots 
N|               light this part of the room. There is a small control panel 
N|               on the wall.  
O|
N|               Some of the equipment is ancient - but none of it is old. 
N|               All of it glistens with the affection of its owner.
O|
N|               There is absolutely nothing in the room to alarm anyone 
N|               except an adult... the kind who starts to wonder who paid 
N|               for it all.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT of Mark. He stands by the lighting panel, 
N|               watching Helen.
O|
N,G|               From his POV WE SEE her brushing the hair out of her eyes 
N|               as she looks slowly round. He brushes the hair out of his. 
N|               For a moment she turns her back to him. He presses a switch 
N|               on the wall. A gentle light ripples through the back of 
N|               Helen's hair.
O|
N|               SHOT of Helen. She turns to him. She is very nearly at a 
N|               loss for words.
O|
